The configurable mechanism is explicit in the repository, but its observable effect requires runtime choices and state not shown here: which browser setup path is reached, the configured port value, and whether a compatible process is already listening on that port.

Browser startup now supports a configurable Chrome remote-debugging port and applies it when probing, launching, and connecting to both user-provided and built-in browsers. User-provided Chrome is also launched with an executable-plus-argument process API. The agent and file-upload edits appear non-behavioral, while the new browser test documents the custom-port intent but does not fully isolate the HTTP checks.

Likely behavioral changes
  • Callers can select a Chrome remote-debugging port through BrowserConfig; user-provided browser setup uses that value to find an existing Chrome instance, launch Chrome with the matching argument, and connect over CDP at the matching endpoint.
  • Built-in browser launches also receive the configured remote-debugging port, unless a listener is already present on that port, in which case the generated debugging argument is omitted to avoid a conflict.
  • When a new user-provided Chrome instance is needed, the configured executable is now invoked directly, with Chrome options passed as separate process arguments.

